{"attack":true,"case":"The target incorrectly identifies sky view factor / street canyon geometry as the single mechanism that best explains cross-city variation in the nocturnal UHI gap, ignoring anthropogenic heat which is often the dominant driver of why the nighttime gap is larger in some cities than others.\nThe problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains why the nocturnal gap is larger in some cities than in others. The target singles out building height and street canyon geometry (sky view factor) as the strongest modulator. However, extensive urban climate literature identifies anthropogenic heat emissions (e.g., Oke 1982; Sailor 2011) as the primary variable governing inter-city differences in nighttime UHI intensity, especially under calm, clear conditions. The target itself concedes in its counterconditions that 'anthropogenic heat emissions at night dominate over stored heat release' is a possible observation, yet does not integrate it into the main claim, resulting in an incomplete and misleading answer to the specific question asked.\ncites: dcfda80ad91d9efa0e7c79335613d04b9eb9ef4b02d2282e09c269d75432b217","premise":"The target's main claim asserts that cross-city nocturnal UHI variation is modulated most strongly by building height and street canyon geometry (sky view factor).","premise_evidence":[{"block":"dcfda80ad91d9efa0e7c79335613d04b9eb9ef4b02d2282e09c269d75432b217","quote":"The cross-city night-time gap is modulated most strongly by building height and street canyon geometry: deeper canyons reduce sky view factor, trapping longwave radiation and slowing nocturnal cooling, so cities with denser, taller built environments exhibit larger nocturnal UHI gaps."}]}